---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Nav from "../../components/Nav.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<BaseLayout title="Atlas — Sample Case Study" description="A structured case-study demo showing context, constraints, decisions and outcomes.">
  <Nav />
  <main class="case-main">
    <section class="case-hero wrap">
      <a class="back-link" href={base}>← Back to portfolio</a>
      <div class="case-kicker"><span>CASE STUDY / 01</span><span>2026</span></div>
      <h1>Atlas</h1>
      <p>A neutral case-study page showing how a portfolio can communicate thinking instead of only showing polished screenshots.</p>
      <div class="case-meta">
        <div><small>Role</small><strong>Product / Systems</strong></div>
        <div><small>Duration</small><strong>8 weeks</strong></div>
        <div><small>Outcome</small><strong>−42% task time</strong></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-grid wrap">
      <aside class="case-sidebar">
        <nav>
          <a href="#context">01 Context</a>
          <a href="#constraints">02 Constraints</a>
          <a href="#decisions">03 Decisions</a>
          <a href="#outcome">04 Outcome</a>
          <a href="#learning">05 Learning</a>
        </nav>
      </aside>
      <article class="case-content">
        <section id="context">
          <span>01 / CONTEXT</span>
          <h2>The problem was not a missing feature. It was accumulated complexity.</h2>
          <p>The previous workflow had grown through small additions. Each feature made sense locally, but the complete journey required too many decisions, too many context switches and too much hidden knowledge.</p>
          <div class="case-visual">
            <div><small>Before</small><strong>14</strong><span>steps</span></div>
            <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
            <div><small>After</small><strong>8</strong><span>steps</span></div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="constraints">
          <span>02 / CONSTRAINTS</span>
          <h2>What could not change.</h2>
          <ul class="constraint-list">
            <li><b>01</b><div><strong>No backend rewrite</strong><p>The redesign had to work with the existing service boundaries.</p></div></li>
            <li><b>02</b><div><strong>Keyboard-first users</strong><p>Power users could not lose speed while new users gained clarity.</p></div></li>
            <li><b>03</b><div><strong>Incremental rollout</strong><p>The new workflow needed to coexist with the previous one.</p></div></li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="decisions">
          <span>03 / DECISION LOG</span>
          <h2>Decisions deserve their own documentation.</h2>
          <div class="decision-card">
            <header><strong>ADR-003</strong><span>Accepted</span></header>
            <h3>Progressive disclosure over a single dense form</h3>
            <p><b>Context:</b> expert users wanted speed while new users needed guidance.</p>
            <p><b>Alternatives:</b> one long form, wizard, command-only interface.</p>
            <p><b>Decision:</b> preserve direct keyboard actions while grouping secondary options behind contextual controls.</p>
            <p><b>Trade-off:</b> slightly more implementation complexity in exchange for lower cognitive load.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="outcome">
          <span>04 / OUTCOME</span>
          <h2>Measure the change, not the aesthetics.</h2>
          <div class="outcome-grid">
            <div><strong>−42%</strong><span>median task time</span></div>
            <div><strong>−31%</strong><span>support requests</span></div>
            <div><strong>+18%</strong><span>feature adoption</span></div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="learning">
          <span>05 / LEARNING</span>
          <h2>What would change next time.</h2>
          <p>The strongest improvement came from removing decisions, not adding UI. The next iteration would instrument hesitation points earlier instead of waiting for usability sessions to expose them.</p>
          <a class="button secondary" href={base}>Return home ↗</a>
        </section>
      </article>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
